{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_5whfnjn6ppwozccwdgbaee76xqrn2j44pbmbsicqghwu2st6swwa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## Outcome\n\n**The Rust translation is correct — I found no divergence and changed no translation code.** The library is a single function, `custom_strdup`, and `src/lib.rs` already matched the C exactly (including the `size_t` wrapping `+1`, which I confirmed against the disassembly: `add $0x1,%rax`).\n\n### Phase A — artifacts (`translation/`)\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly 1 symbol (`custom_strdup`); Rust `.so` exports it. **0 missing, 0 undefined non-libc.** `c_src` is one header + one `.c` file, so there was no untranslated module — nothing to stub or back-fill.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 2 real rejection paths (`if(!str)`, `if(!newstr)`) plus 6 generic FFI-boundary rows. The grep inventory records the *zeros* too: 0 error enums, 0 asserts, 0 range checks, 0 `errno` writes.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 14 rows. There are no option flags, so the axes are input *shapes*: length classes, byte alphabets, alignment, page placement, call multiplicity.\n\n### Phases B & C — 23 differential tests, all passing\nEvery test loads **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and calls only the exported symbol, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is under test. Randomized rows use a fixed seed.\n\n### Phase D — parity across every configuration\n`run_all.sh` enumerates feature combos from `Cargo.toml` (no `[features]` table → `default` + `--no-default-features`), and runs symbol parity + all tests against **both the debug and release cdylib**, so optimized codegen is covered too. Result: `ALL CHECKS PASSED`.\n\n### Two things worth flagging\n1. **My first negative control was a false positive, and I caught it.** All mutants \"failed\" in 0.00s — because the test process couldn't read `$TMPDIR`, so *every* test was dying at `dlopen`, not on the injected bug. Had I trusted the red/green signal, I'd have wrongly concluded the suite had teeth. I rebuilt the mutants inside the readable tree; `mutation_check.sh` now genuinely kills 7/7 mutants (aliasing, off-by-one, invented empty-string rejection, wrong NULL sentinel, invented length cap, high-bit corruption, Rust global allocator) while the real translation passes.\n2. **The malloc-failure test earned its keep.** Mutant m2 — a plausible translation using `Vec`/the Rust global allocator — is killed *only* by `malloc_failure.rs`. Every happy-path test passes against it, and even the `free()`-compatibility test passes, because Rust's allocator is `malloc` underneath at those sizes. The divergence only appears under allocation failure, where `Vec` **aborts the process** instead of returning `NULL`. That's exactly the blind spot Phase C exists for, so I drove it deterministically by lowering `RLIMIT_AS` (soft only, restored after) around a 96 MiB input; the test asserts C actually returned `NULL` as a precondition, so it can't silently degrade into a no-op.\n\nTwo caveats on my setup, for transparency: crates.io was unreachable, so I added `translation/.cargo/config.toml` with `offline = true` and resolved `libloading` 0.8.9 / `libc` 0.2.189 from the local cargo cache — remove that file if you build with network access. I also added `libc` as a dev-dependency (for `mmap`/`mprotect`/`setrlimit` in the guard-page and malloc-failure tests) beyond the `libloading` you specified. I did not modify anything in `c_src/` apart from creating the `build/` directory the task prescribes."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":838,"cache_read_input_tokens":84812,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":838,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"fcfaa9e5-b957-4ddb-8b19-7b18c531ad0f","uuid":"27854c0f-